#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184968} :activity/id #uuid "d3825ee6-b390-5321-ae3c-5fb0645c7b7b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049462 :topic/id #uuid "f7488799-2322-5ff9-8065-6852954f5eb5" :topic/math-academy-id 3819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185322 :tutorial/content "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A= [\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." :tutorial/id #uuid "6230f35e-b526-5e84-be30-86c8cdcbd301" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4891 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f6870e18-6c9b-57a7-81ca-e24f2af2e69d" :step/math-academy-id 30545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184095 :question/id #uuid "fcd3c46b-a87f-5ca8-9cf5-d9444a9c8218" :question/math-academy-id "e-8298"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368899}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "92a4b43a-dde9-5e42-8877-3e32ba9082f1" :step/math-academy-id 30988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184096 :question/id #uuid "2b9ef509-4165-5772-81c1-9a3d78424dd1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8299"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184876} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921861848